   
2946 Problem:-
You are given an m x n integer matrix mat and an integer k. The matrix rows are 0-indexed.
The following proccess happens k times:
Even-indexed rows (0, 2, 4, ...) are cyclically shifted to the left.
Odd-indexed rows (1, 3, 5, ...) are cyclically shifted to the right.
Return true if the final modified matrix after k steps is identical to the original matrix, and false otherwise.

Example 1:
Input: mat = [[1,2,3],[4,5,6],[7,8,9]], k = 4
Output: false
Explanation:
In each step left shift is applied to rows 0 and 2 (even indices), and right shift to row 1 (odd index).

Example 2:
Input: mat = [[1,2,1,2],[5,5,5,5],[6,3,6,3]], k = 2
Output: true
Explanation:

Example 3:
Input: mat = [[2,2],[2,2]], k = 3
Output: true
Explanation:
As all the values are equal in the matrix, even after performing cyclic shifts the matrix will remain the same.

Constraints:-
1 <= mat.length <= 25
1 <= mat[i].length <= 25
1 <= mat[i][j] <= 25
1 <= k <= 50

========================================================================================================================================================================

Solution:-
Intuition
Each row of the matrix undergoes cyclic shifts independently. Instead of simulating k shifts step-by-step, observe that after n (number of columns) shifts, 
a row returns to its original form.
So, we only need to consider k % n shifts.
Even-indexed rows → left shift
Odd-indexed rows → right shift
If after applying these shifts, every element maps back to its original position, the matrix remains unchanged.

Approach
Let m = number of rows, n = number of columns.
Reduce k using k %= n.
Traverse each cell (i, j):
For even rows → expected position = (j + k) % n
For odd rows → expected position = (j - k + n) % n
Compare mat[i][j] with mat[i][expected position]
If any mismatch → return false
If all match → return true

Complexity
Time complexity: O(m × n)
Space complexity: O(1)

Code:-
class Solution {
    public boolean areSimilar(int[][] mat, int k) {
        int m = mat.length;
        int n = mat[0].length;

        k %= n; //(reduce k<n)

        for (int i = 0; i < m; i++) {
            for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
                if (i % 2 == 0) {
                    // even row , left shift
                    if (mat[i][j] != mat[i][(j + k) % n]) {
                        return false;
                    }
                } else {
                    // odd row , right shift
                    if (mat[i][j] != mat[i][(j - k + n) % n]) {
                        return false;
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        return true;
    }
}
